Strategy / matrix builder
A matrix runs the same job several times with different values, for example once per Python version or operating system. JobBuilder.strategy() returns a StrategyBuilder. matrix sets the lists of values to combine, include adds one extra combination and exclude removes one, fail_fast(False) keeps the other combinations running when one fails, and max_parallel limits how many run at the same time. Steps read the current values as expressions such as ${{ matrix.python-version }}. end_strategy() returns to the job.


source

StrategyBuilder
def StrategyBuilder(
    _job:Any, _matrix:dict[str, list[typing.Any]]=&lt;factory&gt;, _fail_fast:bool | None=None,
    _max_parallel:int | None=None, _include:list[dict]=&lt;factory&gt;, _exclude:list[dict]=&lt;factory&gt;
)-&gt;None:
Builder for a job’s strategy block, which runs the job once per combination of matrix values.

Job builder
JobBuilder describes one job. WorkflowBuilder.job(job_id) creates it. The id is the key the job appears under in the YAML, and the name other jobs use to wait for it in needs.


source

JobBuilder
def JobBuilder(
    job_id:str, workflow
):
Fluent builder for a single workflow job.



Job-level fields
Every job needs runs_on, the label of the runner such as ubuntu-latest. needs makes the job wait until other jobs have succeeded. if_, timeout_minutes and continue_on_error work as they do for steps. env sets variables for all the job’s steps, and permissions sets what the job’s GITHUB_TOKEN may do. outputs publishes values that later jobs read as needs.&lt;job&gt;.outputs.&lt;name&gt;. concurrency stops two runs of the job in the same group from overlapping. services starts helper containers beside the job, such as a database for integration tests, and container runs the job’s steps inside a Docker image. environment ties the job to a GitHub environment, which can hold its own secrets and can require someone’s approval before the job runs.


